{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "In `for k in scores:`, what is k on each pass?",
      "options": [
        "A key of scores, the next one each time",
        "A value of scores, the next one each time",
        "A whole key: value pair",
        "How many pairs scores has"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "player = {\"name\": \"Tommy\", \"city\": \"Vice City\"}\nfor i in player:\n    print(i)",
      "options": [
        "Tommy\nVice City",
        "name\ncity",
        "name: Tommy\ncity: Vice City",
        "{'name': 'Tommy', 'city': 'Vice City'}"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "Inside `for name in scores:`, how do you get the score that goes with each name?",
      "options": ["scores.name", "name[scores]", "scores(name)", "scores[name]"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "What does `.items()` give a for loop on each pass?",
      "options": [
        "Only the key",
        "The key and its value, together",
        "Only the value",
        "The position of the pair in the dictionary"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "Which loop gives the same keys as `for k in player:`?",
      "options": [
        "for k in player.values():",
        "for k in player.items():",
        "for k in player.keys():",
        "for k in len(player):"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "prices is `{\"bread\": 3, \"milk\": 2}`. What does a loop over `prices.values()` give?",
      "options": [
        "\"bread\", then \"milk\"",
        "5",
        "\"bread\" with 3, then \"milk\" with 2",
        "3, then 2"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "In `for key, value in player.items():`, player is `{\"name\": \"Tommy\", \"health\": 100}`. What is value on the first pass?",
      "options": ["\"name\"", "100", "\"Tommy\"", "\"health\""],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "ages = {\"Ana\": 12, \"Rex\": 30}\nfor age, name in ages.items():\n    print(name)",
      "options": [
        "12\n30",
        "Ana\nRex",
        "It stops with an error: the names are the wrong way round",
        "age\nname"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "player has 3 key: value pairs. What does `len(player)` give?",
      "options": ["6", "2", "1", "3"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "In the lesson, after counting the letters of \"banana\", what is `counter[\"a\"]`?",
      "options": ["1", "6", "3", "2"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "In the letter counter, why does a letter seen for the first time get `counter[letter] = 1`?",
      "options": [
        "Because counter[letter] + 1 would fail: the letter isn't a key in counter yet",
        "So that letter is counted twice",
        "Because every dictionary starts from 1",
        "So the loop skips that letter"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "counter = {}\nfor letter in \"moon\":\n    counter[letter] = counter[letter] + 1\nprint(counter)",
      "options": [
        "It prints {'m': 1, 'o': 2, 'n': 1}",
        "It prints {'m': 0, 'o': 1, 'n': 0}",
        "It prints {}",
        "It stops with a KeyError: \"m\" isn't a key in counter yet"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "word = \"hello\"\ncounter = {}\nfor letter in word:\n    if letter in counter:\n        counter[letter] = counter[letter] + 1\n    else:\n        counter[letter] = 1\nprint(counter)",
      "options": [
        "{'h': 1, 'e': 1, 'l': 1, 'o': 1}",
        "{'hello': 1}",
        "{'h': 1, 'e': 1, 'l': 2, 'o': 1}",
        "{'l': 2}"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "Why does the sum of a dictionary's scores loop over `.values()` and not over the dictionary itself?",
      "options": [
        "Looping over the dictionary gives the keys, the names, not the scores",
        "A loop over a dictionary works only with .values()",
        "So it gets the scores from the largest to the smallest",
        ".values() adds the scores up by itself"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "fuel = {\"left\": 40, \"right\": 20}\ntotal = 0\nfor f in fuel.values():\n    total = total + f\nprint(total / len(fuel))",
      "options": ["30", "60", "30.0", "2"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "In the Crew Scores mission, why does the loop that finds the top go through the names, not through `.values()`?",
      "options": [
        "With .values() the loop would skip the last member",
        "It needs the name of the best member, and from a name it can get the score too",
        ".values() doesn't work on a dictionary of numbers",
        "So it prints the names in order"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "In the Crew Scores mission, the dictionary has 10 members. How many lines does the program print?",
      "options": ["13", "23", "20", "10"],
      "answer": 1
    },
